Show only the visible part of a clipped Wayland native view, and commit the app's buffers through GPUI.

What: a view its element's clip cuts (scrolled partly out of a container) shows only the part the clip leaves, at its
full size and position, as on macOS, iOS and Windows: the subsurface covers that part, and `wp_viewport.set_source`
crops the app's buffer to match. A source past the buffer's edge ends the app's connection, so the app commits every
buffer through `gpui_linux::NativeViewBuffers::of(&view)` (Send, from any thread): `commit_buffer(&surface, &buffer,
size)` and GPUI's placing commits take turns under one lock, each setting the source for the buffer it commits with
(`Committed::NotShown` while the view isn't painted: nothing attached). 0052's `NativeView::platform_attachment` is
how `of` reaches the view. GPUI sets the view's buffer scale to 1 and the normal transform when it attaches it, as the
crop is in buffer pixels. `gpui_linux` exports `NativeViewBuffers` and `Committed`. `NativeView::new`'s doc says the app commits every buffer
through it, from a thread it stops before GPUI's connection closes. Table tests:
`clipped_views_show_their_visible_part` and `sources_stay_inside_the_buffer`, plus a crop row in 0053's `steps`
(the crop is new code: Chromium gets its crop rects from its own compositor).

Why: without the crop, a view scrolled partly out of its container draws over whatever lies outside the clip.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

